 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/gates/private.ts",
  "line": 170,
  "title": "Keep the percent-decoded layer before backslash decoding: a private value containing a literal `\\/` is no longer matched",
  "detail": "Thank you: review confirmed the decoder matches the regex oracle and is linear, the cache and layer dedupe change nothing, every credential spelling main redacted is still redacted, and the benchmark holds (max +9.8% on one cell under load, private matching about 55% faster on plain logs).\n\nOne privacy regression, reproduced on this PR and on main: `decoded` now feeds percent decoding straight into `decodeBackslashes`, and `normalizedLayers` keeps only the result of both. The spelling that percent decoding alone produced is never checked. With the entry `host\\/path.example` (a literal backslash before the slash), `privateMatches(\"host%5c/path.example\", entries)` returns the entry on main and `[]` on this PR. Through the real `privateOutputData`, main withholds it and this PR publishes it.\n\nFix: keep the percent-decoded text as its own layer in `normalizedLayers`, before backslash decoding, beside the fully decoded layer, so no spelling main checked is lost. Keep the benchmark within 110% of main after the extra layer and update the table in the PR. Add this test from review (it fails on this PR and passes on main):\n\n```ts\ntest(\"percent-decoded private values retain literal backslashes before slashes\", () => {\n  const entries = [{ value: String.raw`host\\/path.example`, entry: 1 }];\n  const text = \"host%5c/path.example\";\n  expect(privateMatches(text, entries)).toEqual(entries);\n});\n```\n\nAlso add a differential test: for every string up to 5 characters over `% 5 c \\ / u 0 a h`, embedded in `host<s>path.example` against several entries including ones that contain `\\/` and `%`, this PR's `privateMatches` returns every entry that main's (frozen inline copy of main's `decoded`) returns."
 }
]
